 "rulebook": "No rule book: a wall-ball drill (throw and catch against a wall, as used in baseball, cricket and handball training) with this benchmark's numbers. The ball is the ITF's (Rules of Tennis 2024, Rule 3 and Appendix I: size, mass, rebound)",
 "deviations": [
  "The hand throws and catches by a grip switched on and off: it holds a ball whose surface is within 3.5 cm of the palm and that moves no faster than 6 m/s relative to it; the ball is released when the grip is switched off.",
  "The wall is rigid and smooth; the ball's rebound from it is the ITF ball's rebound from a hard floor (0.745 of the speed across the wall).",
  "Flight: quasi-steady aerodynamics (MuJoCo's ellipsoid fluid model: drag and Magnus lift), still air."
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Throw the tennis ball in your right hand at the target circle on the wall and catch the rebound with a hand before it touches the ground, and keep hold of it.",
  "rules": "The tennis ball (0.067 m across, 0.0577 kg, it keeps 0.745 of its speed in a bounce, it flies in air with drag) starts gripped against your right palm. A wall stands 5.0 m beyond the line you stay behind; the target is a circle 0.5 m across painted on it, its centre 1.5 m above the ground and 0.0 m to the left of the point straight ahead of you. Let go of the ball (the throw, once); the first thing it touches must be the wall, at a point inside the target circle. Then catch the rebound: a hand's grip closing on the ball in the air (the grip holds a ball whose surface is within 3.5 cm of the palm and that moves no faster than 6 m/s relative to it); either hand may catch, and before the grip the rebound may touch only your hands and forearms. You succeed when the ball has been gripped after the rebound and kept for 1.0 s without a break. You fail if any part of you touches the ground on or beyond the line at any time; if the ball touches the ground at any time before it is caught; if it hits the wall outside the circle; if any part of you touches it after the release and before the wall (your throwing arm may still brush it during the first 0.15 s; it cannot be gripped then); if the rebound touches any part of you other than a hand or a forearm before it is gripped; if you let go of it after the catch; if you fall (your pelvis lower than 0.5 m above the ground, or any part of you other than your feet on the ground); or after 15 s.",
  "venue": "You face the wall (+x), your pelvis 0.40 m behind the line; the wall is 5.0 m beyond the line, 6 m wide and 4 m high. The target circle is straight ahead. The ball is against your right palm, the forearm level and pointing forwards. Still air. The ball is released when the hand's grip is switched off.",
  "difficulty": "To come back five metres through the air the ball has to hit the wall at about 16 m/s or more, inside a circle half a metre across, and it then returns in under half a second at about 10 m/s. The palm must be on its line within a few centimetres with the grip closing as it arrives."
 },
